:"MDU6TGFiZWw4NTgzMjc1MDQ=","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/neovim/neovim/labels/core","name":"core","color":"c5def5","default":false,"description":"Nvim core functionality or code"}],"state":"closed","locked":false,"assignee":null,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":3,"created_at":"2020-04-22T22:02:42Z","updated_at":"2020-04-27T01:02:51Z","closed_at":"2020-04-27T01:02:35Z","author_association":"NONE","type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"\n### Actual behaviour\n:norm works fine and successfully but slow on large datasets (in my case 12k lines with a simple insert to the beginning of the line...\n### Requested behaviour\n:norm runs optionally on multiple threads.\n\n###Background\nI know that there are special tools for finding duplicate files but i decided to do it in nvim. I run :!$find . -type f\nfollowed by :%norm Isha257sum \nin order to hash all my files and find the dupes. that means in my case adding a string to the beginning of 12k lines and it takes forever to do that (adding the string not the actual hashing). \ninterrestingly running :%s/257/256/g \nto correct my typo runs very fast while it essentially runs on a just as big dataset (and basically i could propably just replace the start of a line with the start of the line followed by sha256sum to begin with but thats a different story)","closed_by":{"login":"justinmk","id":1359421,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzNTk0MjE=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1359421?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/justinmk","html_url":"https://github.com/justinmk","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/justinmk/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/justinmk/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/justinmk/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.git